WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.485
A small local model just drove a browser and wrote a web

00:00:03.485 --> 00:00:04.480
page on a laptop.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.221
Should it get the next step too?

00:00:10.373 --> 00:00:11.253
Hi, I'm Peregrin!

00:00:11.253 --> 00:00:14.479
A viral clip showed a tiny model doing real agent work.

00:00:14.479 --> 00:00:17.998
Here is the router built to decide when that is good enough.

00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:19.995
Chapter one.

00:00:19.995 --> 00:00:21.314
The model in the clip.

00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:30.605
The launcher names the model: LFM3, one point seven billion parameters, in

00:00:30.605 --> 00:00:33.019
eight bits, with Pi open beside it.

00:00:33.019 --> 00:00:34.398
This is not ARC.

00:00:34.398 --> 00:00:36.467
ARC cannot write a single word.

00:00:36.887 --> 00:00:38.113
Pi runs the tools.

00:00:38.113 --> 00:00:41.791
The model only emits a call to write, with the whole HTML

00:00:41.791 --> 00:00:45.469
file as its argument, and Pi turns that string into a file.

00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:50.527
It checks its own work with a second tool call, then reports.

00:00:50.527 --> 00:00:52.623
A real loop, from a model this small.

00:00:53.043 --> 00:00:56.301
But it never opened the file it was asked to open, and

00:00:56.301 --> 00:00:58.473
one line of its CSS is silently broken.

00:00:58.473 --> 00:01:00.102
The user had to ask again.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:02.669
Chapter two.

00:01:02.669 --> 00:01:04.215
Why small errors add up.

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:11.247
Agent runs fail by compounding.

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.763
Call q the chance that one step goes right.

00:01:14.713 --> 00:01:18.308
A run of n dependent steps finishes only if every one of

00:01:18.308 --> 00:01:20.105
them does, so the chances multiply.

00:01:21.342 --> 00:01:25.610
At ninety-five percent a step, a twenty-step task finishes about thirty-six

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.161
percent of the time.

00:01:27.161 --> 00:01:29.101
At ninety-nine percent, about eighty-two.

00:01:29.101 --> 00:01:31.429
The gap between models lives here.

00:01:33.425 --> 00:01:34.029
Chapter three.

00:01:34.029 --> 00:01:35.238
A router in front.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:44.298
ARC reads the agent's conversation through the first twelve layers of a

00:01:44.298 --> 00:01:46.880
small Qwen model, left exactly as they were.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.063
Small heads read that state and predict, for every candidate model, its

00:01:51.063 --> 00:01:55.140
chance of success and how many tokens it will use, in four kinds.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.654
Only when a developer asks a multiple-choice question does the input run

00:01:59.654 --> 00:02:02.724
on through the upper twelve layers, which were fine-tuned.

00:02:04.992 --> 00:02:08.099
Picture the clip's job as a row of steps, all handed to

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:09.652
the small model on the laptop.

00:02:10.072 --> 00:02:13.345
Before each step, ARC predicts how likely the local model is

00:02:13.345 --> 00:02:14.535
to get it right.

00:02:14.535 --> 00:02:17.510
Reading the stats page and planning the report score lowest.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:21.117
With the prefer-local rule, every step over the zero point eight

00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:22.276
five line stays local.

00:02:22.276 --> 00:02:24.884
The two under it go to a cloud model.

00:02:25.304 --> 00:02:28.228
So the cloud bill only lands on the steps that need it,

00:02:28.228 --> 00:02:31.153
and those are the steps where a small model's slips would compound.

00:02:32.608 --> 00:02:35.348
Away from the laptop, the utility rule chooses.

00:02:35.768 --> 00:02:38.463
p is ARC's predicted chance that a model succeeds.

00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:41.860
C is its predicted tokens, priced at your own rates.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.349
ARC never learns a price.

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:46.661
V is what a success is worth to you.

00:02:46.661 --> 00:02:50.517
The Sage preset sets four dollars fifty; the Swift preset sets ten

00:02:50.517 --> 00:02:53.088
cents, and the cheapest model nearly always wins.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:55.599
Chapter four.

00:02:55.599 --> 00:02:56.696
Does it hold up?

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:06.459
Here is the hybrid setup against Opus five point five on six benchmarks.

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:08.494
Quality holds on code and science.

00:03:08.914 --> 00:03:13.259
The bottom row is the share of tasks sent to the local model.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:17.604
But that local model is DeepSeek Flash, spread over several DGX Spark boxes.

00:03:17.604 --> 00:03:18.606
Not a laptop.

00:03:19.925 --> 00:03:25.046
Averaging the card's own per-benchmark ratios gives ninety-eight point five percent, not

00:03:25.046 --> 00:03:27.179
the headline's ninety-seven point five.

00:03:27.179 --> 00:03:29.739
Close, but not reproducible as stated.

00:03:31.192 --> 00:03:33.004
Three places the claim does not reach.

00:03:33.503 --> 00:03:36.901
None of the eleven models it names runs on a laptop, and

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:38.601
LFM3 is not one of them.

00:03:39.021 --> 00:03:43.179
On the one customer-service benchmark, success drops from forty-two point eight

00:03:43.179 --> 00:03:44.691
to thirty-five point three.

00:03:45.111 --> 00:03:48.970
And its question head changes its answer when the options are reordered

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.542
nineteen percent of the time, twice Jev's rate.

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:56.733
A small agent model is useful when something judges it step by step.

00:03:56.733 --> 00:03:59.174
ARC is the right shape for that judge.

00:03:59.174 --> 00:04:01.004
Today it routes between cloud models.

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:04.552
The clip is LFM3, not ARC.

00:04:04.552 --> 00:04:07.792
ARC predicts success and tokens, and you set the prices.

00:04:07.792 --> 00:04:11.356
And its local model lives in a data centre, for now.

00:04:11.776 --> 00:04:13.964
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:13.964 --> 00:04:14.589
I'm Peregrin.

00:04:14.589 --> 00:04:14.902
Bye!
